\f4\fs26 Sarah was home alone when the list of new allocations were released. She did not want any company for this, the lowest point of her life. In her estimation, throwing her hat in for random allocation had diminished her in every way. Consoling herself she was making the best of a bad of an impossible situation did not help. 
\f5\i Here goes. Fingers crossed.
\f4\i0 \
She waved her hand and watched as the wall screen flicked and flitted, before the image became crisp. Allocations for level 4 had not concluded and as the names, all unknown to her, flashed on the screen she felt overwhelmed by panic - all of these people are going to marry someone they do not know, all for the good of the economy. 
\f5\i Oh dear, so am I. 
\f4\i0 She hoped that her allocation partner, whoever he may be, was going through the same hell. He was not. The randomness and integrity of this allocation had been completely corrupted by a vicious algorithm, smeared with the fingerprints of a well paid computer hacker. \
Then her name appeared on the screen along side of the name of her betrothed, Alexander Shakti L.5. She was motionless for a very long time and then laughed, then cried and laughed again. She would not have imagined her situation could get any worse. It now infinitely worse. \
Her holo-phone rang. She did not answer it then or for the rest of the day. messages piled up in her in-box. When darkness arrived there was a knock at the front door. 
\f5\i A knock? She thought, how did anyone get close to my front door without being trounced by security? Why wouldn\'92t they use the doorbell? 
\f4\i0 With curiosity taking control, she finally uncoiled from the foetal position and hobbled on numb legs to the door. The one-way view panel showed a slender looking woman, in her late 50\'92s, her thin lips, painted red, pursed closely together. She was sensibly dressed in a subtle yellow dress suit with matching shoes and handbag. \
As Sarah opened the door, the woman stepped back, allowing a large man standing at the side of the doorway, to push his way in. Sarah was knocked backwards. Still upright she stood her ground.\
\'93Don\'92t try it, little lady.\'94 The man warned.\
\'93That\'92s odd. I was about to issue you with the same warning, large oaf.\'94 Sarah shot back, staring him down. He laughed and moved towards her. She was too quick and was twisting his arm behind his back in a flash. \'93Don\'92t fight it.\'94 The giant man\'92s ego refused to obey and he tried to throw himself backwards to break free. Sarah raised his arm and loudly snapped his humerus under the weight of his own body. He screamed and screamed as Sarah threw him to the door. All the while, the lady calmly sat on the lounge, straightening out the hem of her skirt.\
\'93Now, let us talk about you.\'94 Sarah stood squarely in front of the odd lady.\
\'93Well, aren\'92t you just a firebrand.\'94 The woman smiled smugly. \'93I am Eloise Shakti, your future mother-in-contract.\'94\
\'93Was the gorilla necessary?\'94 Sarah was unimpressed with the mother-in-contract so far.\
\'93Forgive me for Benjamin, my body guard. He sometimes gets enthusiastic.\'94 Eloise smiled then her face turned to ash. \'93Sarah Jacobson, Ph.D. Level 5, Cyber Security Manager, currently under contract with the Police - you poor thing. You earn more than my boy, but unlike him, you have real career prospects.\'94\
\'93Yes, I can\'92t wait to get back to profiteering.\'94 She figured she had better sound sufficiently capitalist for her highly regarded mum-to-be.\
\'93Indeed. Alexander said you were a lovely creature, but that does not even begin to describe you, does it? I did not expect you would be so stunning. And you can handle yourself, too, besting Benjamin so easily. So my question is, with all your obvious physical attributes, education and intellectual firepower, what are you doing putting yourself up for random allocation?\'94 It would take a very decent argument to satisfy Eloise that there was nothing suspicious going on here.\
\'93I am not sure I know what you mean. I am 27 and want some certainty in my life.\'94 Sarah hoped that would work, but Eloise tittered quietly, showing her perfect teeth, for just a few seconds before she cut it short and allowed her calm dignity to return. \
\'93You will have to do better than that, my dear. I am not a person to meddle with.\'94 The name of Eloise knew she was the subject of fear and disdain. It pleased her greatly. \
Sarah brain activity trebled. Neurones fired up as she searched the darkest reaches of her mind to come up with a plausible story. She knew that mother Shakti was here to vet her, but after many fictitious scenarios formed in her head, she concluded that she did not care if she was rejected by the Shakti family. In fact, she would be very pleased if they decided to call it off. \'93I am pregnant.\'94\
\'93That makes perfect sense dear. Thank you for the truth. It is a rare commodity\'94\
\'93I expect you will be calling off the allocation.\'94 Sarah was not sad, even if this was the first time she had ever been rejected.\
\'93Not on your life, my dear. I have been waiting years to pass him off to a wife or a husband, I honestly did not care which. He\'92s your problem now, my girl. I just wanted an answer to the question of why someone like you would accept someone like my son. I have my answer. Thank you, dear.\'94\
\'93Should I tell him about the pregnancy?\'94\
\'93Only to tell him that he is the father. You will have to consummate the relationship quickly for him to think it is his. I will accept the child as my grandchild, happy in the knowledge that whatever caused the genetic car-crash that is my Alexander, it will not happen to this child.\'94 She stood and walked to the door. \'93Thank you my dear. I don\'92t think there is any reason to see you before the ceremony, do you?\'94\
\'93Unless you and your family want to get to know me.\'94 Sarah thought they might.\
\'93See? No reason at all. See you at the ceremony.\'94 And she was gone.\
Sarah checked her in-box and deleted all the mail titled \'93SHAKTI? WTF?\'94 She noted a number of messages from Eloise Shakti\'92s son and decided to take her advice. She must consummate the relationship quickly. It seemed very contrived and nothing like what she thought her first time would be like. 
\f5\i I did not think I would be giving up my virginity to such an idiot, but all girls give it up for idiots don\'92t they? 
\f4\i0 When she had imagined having sex for the first time, it would be with a man tall, strong, handsome, funny, intelligent, gentle and kind. Shakti ticked none of the boxes, but she nevertheless contacted him and invited herself to his apartment. \
She returned from his apartment later that evening feeling dirty, even though she had not actually engaged in sex with Shaki. She had managed to convinced convinced him that he had penetrated her, but in reality she had finished him off with a grandiose wank while riding reverse cowgirl. She also managed, she hoped, not to pass on the virus she carried. The last thing the world needed to see was a Super-Shakti. Perfection in idiocy. \
Her first shower took two hours, but she was still unclean and opted for a sauna and then took some steam before having one more shower. As the water trickled down the drain, she was reminded of the shower in the public facility. She enjoyed herself that night, even if the facilities had been disgusting. It seemed an age ago, so much water having passed under the bridge, but it had been less than two months.
